---
// Google tag (gtag.js) for the marketing site (change: marketing-analytics-parity).
//
// This site reports to the SAME GA4 properties as play-web and creator-web, on
// purpose. A visitor's path runs marketing -> creator (build a game) or marketing
// -> player (join one); splitting that across properties would break every funnel
// at exactly the handover the site exists to produce. It also keeps the Privacy
// Policy honest: it names the cookie `_ga_G-89TM5X68RR` to users by name
// (packages/shared/src/legalContent.ts), and a separate property here would set a
// cookie that document never mentions.
//
// The ids are hardcoded rather than read from `astrowind:config`. They used to come
// from `analytics.vendors.googleAnalytics.id`, which was `null`, so this site shipped
// untagged while looking configured. They are public, non-secret identifiers, and
// scripts/test-analytics-gate.ts asserts these literals equal the shared constants,
// so the three surfaces cannot drift apart.
//
// NOT imported from @rushpoint/shared: this app deliberately carries no dependency on
// it (see src/utils/i18n.ts), and an inline classic script cannot import from the
// module graph anyway. The gate is what keeps the duplication honest.
---

{
  /*
    Loaded IMPERATIVELY, never as a static <script async src>: a static tag fires the
    request to Google before any JS can decide, so localhost would still be contacted.
    The early return below is what makes "no request at all on a dev machine" true.
    This component previously used a static tag and had no host rule at all.

    `is:inline` is load-bearing. Without it Astro processes and bundles the script,
    which defers it — and a deferred tag both misses the early page and stops the
    ordering guarantee that <meta charset> wins the document's first 1024 bytes.

    The host rule duplicates shouldLoadAnalytics() in packages/shared/src/analytics.ts
    because an inline classic script cannot import. It is NOT trusted:
    scripts/test-analytics-gate.ts extracts this predicate and executes it against the
    same case table as the shared function, so drift fails `npm test`. Change the rule
    here => change it there (and in play-web/index.html + creator-web/index.html).
  */
}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    if (/^(localhost|127\.0\.0\.1|\[::1\])$/.test(location.hostname)) return;
    var ID = 'G-89TM5X68RR';
    var ID_SECONDARY = 'G-4LELMBZWPZ';
    var s = document.createElement('script');
    s.async = true;
    s.src = 'https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=' + ID;
    document.head.appendChild(s);
    window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
    window.gtag = function () { window.dataLayer.push(arguments); };
    gtag('js', new Date());
    gtag('config', ID, {
      anonymize_ip: true,
      allow_google_signals: false,
      allow_ad_personalization_signals: false
    });
    gtag('config', ID_SECONDARY, {
      anonymize_ip: true,
      allow_google_signals: false,
      allow_ad_personalization_signals: false
    });
  })();
</script>
